        sections: [
          { title: 'Panel Strategies', type: 'rule', content: 'Prepare 3-4 key points you want to make. Listen actively — build on what others say. When asked a question: answer concisely (60-90 seconds max), add ONE original insight, give a concrete example. Building on others: "I completely agree with what [name] said, and I\'d add..." "To build on that point..." "That\'s interesting because in my experience..." Disagreeing: "I see it slightly differently..." "I\'d offer a different perspective..." "That\'s one view, but there\'s another side to this..."' },
          { title: 'Standing Out', type: 'tip', content: 'What makes panellists memorable: one surprising statistic, a personal story that illustrates the point, a framework that helps the audience think differently, a bold (but defensible) claim. What makes panellists forgettable: agreeing with everything, speaking in generalities, using jargon, going on too long. Rule: say less, say it better. One sharp point beats three vague ones.' },
          { title: 'Panel Response', type: 'exercise', question: 'You\'re on a panel about "the future of education." The moderator asks: "Will AI replace teachers?" Give a 90-second answer that stands out.', answer: '"I think asking whether AI will replace teachers is the wrong question — it\'s like asking whether textbooks replaced teachers in the 1500s. They didn\'t. They changed what teachers DO. AI will do the same. What AI can do brilliantly is personalise learning — adjusting pace, difficulty, and style for each student in real time. That\'s something a teacher with 30 students physically cannot do. But what AI cannot do — and I believe never will — is inspire. A great teacher makes you believe you can do things you didn\'t think possible. No algorithm does that. So my answer is: AI will replace the administrative and repetitive parts of teaching, freeing teachers to do what they\'re actually best at — connecting with young people and igniting curiosity. The schools that get this balance right will be extraordinary."', answerExplanation: 'Reframes the question (wrong question — bold), historical analogy (textbooks), concedes AI\'s strength (personalisation), identifies what AI can\'t do (inspire), ends with a forward-looking vision (extraordinary schools). Under 90 seconds, memorable, defensible.' },
        ],
        keyTakeaways: ['60-90 seconds max per answer — say less, say it better', 'One sharp insight beats three vague ones', 'Reframe the question if it\'s too simple: "The real question is..."'],
